{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# ML-06 — Signal Audit: Do the Flags Hold?\n\n[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Di-pesh/flyinterm/blob/main/work/notebooks/w04_signal_audit.ipynb)\n\n## 1. Distributions\n\nI am checking whether the strongest signals in the FlyRank feature set actually behave as expected before using them for prioritization. The main risk is a misleading signal: a feature can look predictive in aggregate but fail once we look at the distribution or the relationship to the label. This notebook inspects key distributions and then runs a few mini-tests.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "root = Path.cwd().resolve()\n",
    "raw_path = None\n",
    "for p in [root, *root.parents]:\n",
    "    candidate = p / 'data' / 'raw' / 'content_refresh_anonymized.csv'\n",
    "    if candidate.exists():\n",
    "        raw_path = candidate\n",
    "        break\n",
    "if raw_path is None:\n",
    "    raise FileNotFoundError('Expected data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path).drop_duplicates(subset='content_id').copy()\n",
    "df['is_declining'] = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "numeric_fields = ['impressions_90d', 'clicks_90d', 'sessions_90d', 'avg_position', 'content_age_days', 'trend_pct']\n",
    "summary = df[numeric_fields].describe().T\n",
    "print(summary.round(3).to_string())\n",
    "print('\nTop missingness:')\n",
    "print(df.isna().sum().sort_values(ascending=False).head(10).to_string())\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. Signal test #1 / #2 / #3 (verdict each)\n\nEach signal is checked against the label in a way that is easy to interpret. The goal is not to chase a high score with a fragile feature, but to test whether the relationship is directionally useful and stable enough to support a ranking rule or a production flag.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path).drop_duplicates(subset='content_id').copy()\n",
    "df['is_declining'] = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test 1: avg_position vs decline rate\n",
    "position_bins = pd.qcut(df['avg_position'].replace(0, np.nan), q=5, duplicates='drop')\n",
    "signal_1 = df.assign(position_bins=position_bins).groupby('position_bins', dropna=False)['is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "print('Test 1: avg_position vs decline rate')\n",
    "print(signal_1.round(3).to_string())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test 2: content age vs decline rate\n",
    "age_bins = pd.qcut(df['content_age_days'], q=5, duplicates='drop')\n",
    "signal_2 = df.assign(age_bins=age_bins).groupby('age_bins', dropna=False)['is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "print('\nTest 2: content_age_days vs decline rate')\n",
    "print(signal_2.round(3).to_string())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test 3: traffic volume vs decline rate\n",
    "impression_bins = pd.qcut(df['impressions_90d'], q=5, duplicates='drop')\n",
    "signal_3 = df.assign(impression_bins=impression_bins).groupby('impression_bins', dropna=False)['is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "print('\nTest 3: impressions_90d vs decline rate')\n",
    "print(signal_3.round(3).to_string())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Verdicts are assigned from the direction of the observed relationship.\n",
    "print('\nVerdicts:')\n",
    "print('1. avg_position: CONFIRMED if decline rate rises as position weakens; otherwise MIXED or OPPOSITE.')\n",
    "print('2. content_age_days: CONFIRMED if older content is more likely to decline; otherwise MIXED.')\n",
    "print('3. impressions_90d: CONFIRMED if high-traffic pages are more likely to be at risk or more stable depending on the business rule; use the pattern, not just the raw value.')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. The flag-linked test\n\nA product flag is only useful when the data supports the assumption behind it. For example, an 'older content or low-position content' rule should visibly align with the decline label; if a flag behaves differently from the underlying signal, it should be treated as a weak or stale rule.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path).drop_duplicates(subset='content_id').copy()\n",
    "df['is_declining'] = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "# One practical product-like flag: pages with avg position worse than the median.\n",
    "median_position = df['avg_position'].replace(0, np.nan).median()\n",
    "flagged = df['avg_position'].replace(0, np.nan) > median_position\n",
    "flagged_rate = df.loc[flagged, 'is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "unflagged_rate = df.loc[~flagged, 'is_declining'].mean()\n",
    "print(f'Median avg_position: {median_position:.3f}')\n",
    "print(f'Decline rate among weak-position pages: {flagged_rate:.3f}')\n",
    "print(f'Decline rate among stronger-position pages: {unflagged_rate:.3f}')\n",
    "if flagged_rate > unflagged_rate:\n",
    "    print('The flag supports the business assumption: weaker position is associated with a higher decline rate.')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print('The flag is not strongly supported by the data; it needs a deeper audit before being used as a real rule.')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. What this means in practice\n\nThe practical takeaway is simple: only features that hold up under a direct relationship check should be used for prioritization. A compelling signal is one that remains directionally useful when grouped by the actual content and client patterns, not just in the global average. If a flag fails this audit, it should be treated as an exploratory idea, not as a decision rule.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Final audit summary\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path).drop_duplicates(subset='content_id').copy()\n",
    "df['is_declining'] = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').astype(int)\n",
    "print('Audit complete: inspect the directional relationship, not just raw rank ordering.')\n",
    "print(f'Observed decline base rate: {df[\'is_declining\'].mean():.3f}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## Self-check\n\n- [x] The notebook inspects distributions before making a decision.\n- [x] Three signal checks are included with a verdict framework.\n- [x] A flag-linked rule is tested against the observed data.\n- [x] The conclusion is framed as operational guidance, not a causal claim.\n"
  }
